WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.086
The last video of the course.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:03.867
We end with one question.

3
00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:07.784
How capable are agents, really, and how fast is that changing?

4
00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:15.262
We will answer it with benchmarks, read carefully, because benchmark literacy is a skill the people around you will not have.

5
00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:17.407
Start with the trend.

6
00:00:17.457 --> 00:00:34.036
In March 2025 the research group METR published a way to measure agent capability that is more useful than a score: the length of a task, measured in how long it takes a skilled human, that an agent can complete with 50 percent reliability.

7
00:00:34.086 --> 00:00:41.989
They found that length doubling roughly every seven months since 2019, with signs of a faster pace in the most recent models.

8
00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:44.158
Read the fine print, as always.

9
00:00:44.208 --> 00:00:49.581
These are software tasks, at 50 percent reliability, and the trend line is a forecast.

10
00:00:49.631 --> 00:00:51.027
Forecasts can be wrong.

11
00:00:51.077 --> 00:00:58.979
But as a way of thinking about capability, task length at a stated reliability is exactly the frame this course has used.

12
00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.983
Benchmarks make three kinds of claim.

13
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:07.357
Tasks with tests, in software, maths and puzzles, where a program checks the answer.

14
00:01:07.407 --> 00:01:11.964
Scores climb fastest here, and these are the numbers in launch announcements.

15
00:01:12.014 --> 00:01:22.329
Expert-graded work, such as GDPval, where professionals grade real work products blind; the best models are near parity, on tasks that arrive fully specified.

16
00:01:22.379 --> 00:01:28.471
And interactive tasks, such as τ-bench, with a simulated user, real tools and rules to follow.

17
00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:33.077
Scores are lowest here, and these tasks look most like your project.

18
00:01:33.127 --> 00:01:36.916
When someone quotes a benchmark, ask which kind it is.

19
00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:40.678
You met GDPx in week four.

20
00:01:40.728 --> 00:01:49.863
Partner firms wrote 206 tasks from their own work and graded the results, and the models ran with their own harnesses, as they would for a customer.

21
00:01:49.913 --> 00:01:53.265
The best score in November 2026 was about 16 percent.

22
00:01:53.315 --> 00:01:58.368
Compare that with near parity on GDPval, and the difference is instructive.

23
00:01:58.418 --> 00:02:03.131
GDPval hands the model a complete brief and all the files.

24
00:02:03.181 --> 00:02:09.934
GDPx tasks look more like a job: messy inputs, judgement calls, things the brief did not say.

25
00:02:09.984 --> 00:02:19.120
The gap between those two numbers is the gap this course has been about, and it is where the harness, the evaluation and the people come in.

26
00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.818
So here is the checklist for reading any benchmark claim.

27
00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.217
Who wrote the tasks and who graded them?

28
00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:28.515
Is the score from one run, or pass^k?

29
00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:33.013
Did the task arrive fully specified, or did the agent have to find things out?

30
00:02:33.063 --> 00:02:37.211
Which harness was used, and could the model have seen the test during training?

31
00:02:37.261 --> 00:02:45.307
And the question that matters most to a decision memo: what would this task look like inside your organisation, with your data, your systems and your users?

32
00:02:46.057 --> 00:02:47.758
Next session is the last one.

33
00:02:47.808 --> 00:02:55.053
Ten minutes per team, in front of a panel that plays the buyer, and the panel will ask the four questions from the first video.

34
00:02:55.103 --> 00:02:56.803
What tools may the loop call?

35
00:02:56.853 --> 00:02:58.554
What is it allowed to touch?

36
00:02:58.604 --> 00:03:03.222
How do we know it did the right thing, reliably, and not once in a demo?

37
00:03:03.272 --> 00:03:07.307
And what does it cost, in money and in people's time, to find out?

38
00:03:07.357 --> 00:03:14.602
If your team can answer those four about your own service, with numbers and traces, you have done what this course set out to teach.

39
00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:18.103
Thank you for taking it, and see you at the final presentations.
